{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אם יקום", :translit "im yakum", :gloss "if he rises"} {:he "ירפא לו", :translit "yerape lo", :gloss "he shall heal him"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter alef, forming a frame around the word ירפא לו (he shall heal him)."}], :frame {:text "Three are pardoned for all their sins, one of them being a sick person who recovered; this is derived from Exodus 21:19, ורפא ירפא, understood as 'he shall heal him' — permission is granted to the physician to heal, and his recovery signals atonement (Nedarim 41a). The idea that suffering can serve an atoning function is addressed in Mishnah Eduyot 2:10 and Shabbat 55a.", :sources ["Nedarim 41a" "Mishnah Eduyot 2:10" "Shabbat 55a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ונקה", :translit "venakah", :gloss "and acquitted"} {:he "ונקה המכה", :translit "venakah hamakeh", :gloss "and acquitted the one who struck"} {:he "ונקה האיש מעון", :translit "venakah ha'ish me'aven", :gloss "and acquitted the man from iniquity"} {:he "מי שלח ידו במשיח ה' ונקה", :translit "mi shalach yado bemashiah Hashem venakah", :gloss "who laid his hand on the Lord's anointed and acquitted"} {:he "אם יקום והתהלך בחוץ", :translit "im yakum vehithalech baChutz", :gloss "if he rises and walks outside"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ונקה המכה" "ונקה האיש מעון" "מי שלח ידו במשיח ה' ונקה"]}], :frame {:text "Three are pardoned for all their sins: a sick person who recovered, a bridegroom, and a king (Nedarim 41a). The sick person who recovered is tied to Exodus 21:19, אם יקום והתהלך בחוץ, read as one who has risen from illness and is now acquitted (Nedarim 41a). The bridegroom's atonement is tied to the sotah ritual of Numbers 5:31, where ונקה האיש מעון is understood as the husband being acquitted once he gives his wife the bitter waters, she dies, and he takes another wife (Sotah 28a). The king is tied to 2 Samuel 1:14, where David asks the Amalekite, מי שלח ידו במשיח ה', and the anointed one referred to as king is Saul (2 Samuel 1:14–16).", :sources ["Nedarim 41a" "Sotah 28a" "2 Samuel 1:14–16"]}} {:quotes [{:he "רק שבתו יתן", :translit "rak shivto yiten", :gloss "only his rest he shall give"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The word רק ('only') is read as a limiting exclusion: the assailant pays for rest but is exempt if the injured party disregards the physician's instructions."}], :frame nil}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter alef, forming a frame around the word ירפא לו (he shall heal him).", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ונקה המכה" "ונקה האיש מעון" "מי שלח ידו במשיח ה' ונקה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ונקה המכה", :refs ["Exodus 21:19"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ונקה האיש מעון", :refs ["Numbers 5:31"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "מי שלח ידו במשיח ה' ונקה", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "The word רק ('only') is read as a limiting exclusion: the assailant pays for rest but is exempt if the injured party disregards the physician's instructions.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:18:15.216897352Z", :model "glm-5.2"}